<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anarchism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1840 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anarchism is a political philosophy and movement that seeks to replace the state, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and all coercive hierarchy with voluntary associations. Taking shape in 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with Proudhon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Bakunin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kropotkin">Kropotkin</a>, it flourished until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, was suppressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> and defeated by the communists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and has revived since the 1960s in protest movements.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word comes from the Ancient Greek anarkhia, meaning without a ruler, and appears in English from 1642, when it stressed disorder. Factions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> called their opponents anarchists, although few of those accused shared much with later anarchists. The first political philosopher to call himself an anarchist and give the word a positive meaning was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a>.</p><p>Precursors are found in the ancient world: the Taoist philosophers Zhuang Zhou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the Cynics, the Stoics and tragedians who used the myth of Antigone to show the conflict between state law and personal autonomy. Modern anarchism emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. By the late 1870s several schools were well defined, and the movement spread across the world from 1880 to 1914.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Anarchism seeks to abolish institutions that perpetuate authority, coercion or hierarchy, above all the state and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, and to replace them with stateless societies of voluntary associations based on free association and reciprocity. It is counted as part of the libertarian wing of the socialist movement.</p><p>Its schools are grouped into two traditions. Individualist anarchism stresses freedom from restraint on the individual, while social anarchism seeks the free potential of society through equality and social ownership. Mutualism, developed by Proudhon, aims at reciprocity, federation and a bank of the people; in What Is Property? (1840) he described a third form of society, the synthesis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and property. Collectivist anarchism, associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a>, advocates collective ownership of the means of production, to be won by violent revolution. The communist and syndicalist currents followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Anarchists took part in revolutions, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, whose end marked the close of the classical era. In that era some anarchists used assassination as propaganda of the deed, mostly in the late 1870s, 1880s and 1890s, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-goldman">Emma Goldman</a> and Errico Malatesta defended limited violence as a necessary reaction to state violence. Others took the path of nonviolence. Francisco Ferrer founded the Escuela Moderna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1901, a secular school that the state harassed until it closed. In the last decades of the 20th century and the 21st the movement has revived within anti-capitalist, anti-war and anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/globalisation">globalisation</a> movements.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anarchism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cai Yuanpei, Li Shizeng and Wu Zhihui developed a Chinese variety of <b>anarchism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/new-culture-movement">New Culture Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>At various times Surrealists aligned with communism and <b>anarchism</b>, and they looked to the Hegelian and Marxist dialectic.</blockquote><a href="/movement/surrealism">Surrealism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Elsewhere the word still often means <b>anarchism</b> or libertarian socialism, and the free-market form is often called liberalism, for example in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/libertarianism">Libertarianism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 19th century social democrat was a catch-all name for international socialists who followed Lassalle or Marx, as against the utopian socialists, and it covered revolutionary and non-revolutionary currents alike, though not <b>anarchism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-democracy">Social democracy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
